{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>See, You say to me: Lead this people, etc.</b> This is the most difficult passage in the Torah, concerning which all the commentators went out to gather and found no sufficient explanation of what Moses' request truly was — apart from several uncertainties that arise in the language of the dialogue that Moses had with the Holy One, blessed be He. I do not decree and say that my opinion is the most correct of all — Heaven forbid — but I say this: since the commentators are not of one mind, and each one contradicts the words of his fellow and piles upon him bundles of objections, nevertheless the core matters they have established are true in themselves, even if they do not preserve the exact form of the language; I too will answer my portion and say: We find above a doubled matter, for at first the Holy One, blessed be He, said to Moses, ״וְעַתָּה לֵךְ נְחֵה אֶת הָעָם״, and afterward He returned and said, ״לֵךְ עֲלֵה מִזֶּה אַתָּה וְהָעָם״. And apart from the doubling, there are several changes in the language: both from the expression ״נְחֵה״ to ״עֲלֵה״, and His saying ״אַתָּה וְהָעָם״, and what He added in the second instance by saying ״אֲשֶׁר הֶעֱלִיתָ מֵאֶרֶץ מִצְרַיִם״, and besides these, many other changes of language. And regarding the matter of the dispatching of the angel, He said at first ״הִנֵּה מַלְאָכִי יֵלֵךְ לְפָנֶיךָ״, and afterward He said ״וְשָׁלַחְתִּי לְפָנֶיךָ מַלְאָךְ״." "All these precise distinctions are plausible to hear, for in the matter of the calf the mixed multitude was the source of the sin, and Israel were drawn after them. And Moses in his prayer made two parts: he prayed first concerning the children of Israel, whose guilt was not so great, and afterward he prayed also on behalf of the mixed multitude, as is explained above in the pericope of the calf. And correspondingly, when the Holy One, blessed be He, granted his request, He granted it first concerning the children of Israel — not to destroy them — and concerning them He said <b>וְעַתָּה לֵךְ נְחֵה אֶת הָעָם</b>, for the expression ״נְחֵה״ indicates that he would lead them gently and back, and He called them <b>הָעָם</b> by way of rebuke, because they had been enticed by the mixed multitude. And He said <b>אֶל אֲשֶׁר דִּבַּרְתִּי לָךְ</b>, for the Holy One, blessed be He, wished to fulfill His oath to Moses and said <b>וְאֶעֱשֶׂה אוֹתְךָ לְגוֹי גָּדוֹל</b>, which means to give the land to the seed of Moses, as though He spoke to fulfill His promise to Moses. And now He returned and said that that promise which I spoke to make of you — behold, I restore it to Israel; and this is what He said: <b>אֶל אֲשֶׁר דִּבַּרְתִּי לָךְ</b>. And since all the angels of mercy on high were already prepared from the days of the fathers to guard on the way the seed of Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob, therefore He said <b>הִנֵּה מַלְאָכִי יֵלֵךְ לְפָנֶיךָ</b> — there is no need to send another angel, for there is already an angel prepared for this. And concerning Israel He said <b>וּבְיוֹם פָּקְדִי וּפָקַדְתִּי עֲלֵיהֶם</b> etc. — to exact punishment from them little by little." "But concerning the mixed multitude He said, ״וַיִּגֹּף ה׳ אֶת הָעָם עַל אֲשֶׁר עָשׂוּ אֶת הָעֵגֶל״, for we have already said that Israel did not know and did not perceive the stratagems of the mixed multitude, and ״יָדַע שׁוֹר קֹנֵהוּ״ — these are the mixed multitude, as is found in the Midrash (see Vayikra Rabbah 27:8). And afterward HaShem relented from the evil also concerning the mixed multitude, and said, ״לֵךְ עֲלֵה מִזֶּה אַתָּה וְהָעָם אֲשֶׁר הֶעֱלִיתָ מֵאֶרֶץ מִצְרָיִם״. For it is known that Moses did not deliberate in valor, and Moses himself brought them up, and He did not use the expression ״נְחֵה״ which indicates gentle guidance, but rather ״עֲלֵה מִזֶּה אַתָּה וְהָעָם״ — meaning: you go up and bring them up also against their will, for they always choose to return to their source, to Egypt. And He said ״אֶל הָאָרֶץ אֲשֶׁר נִשְׁבַּעְתִּי לְאַבְרָהָם״ etc., and did not say simply ״to their fathers,״ for the mixed multitude are not of their seed. And what He said — ״אֲשֶׁר נִשְׁבַּעְתִּי״ etc. — is to tell you: even though I swore to Abraham, to Isaac, and to Jacob, saying ״to your seed I will give it,״ and they are not of their seed, and if so I am not obligated to bring them into the land by virtue of the oath and the covenant of the fathers — nevertheless I will bring them there for your honor, since you accepted them, as it is written ״אֲשֶׁר הֶעֱלִיתָ״ etc. ״וְשָׁלַחְתִּי מַלְאָךְ לְפָנֶיךָ״ — for they have no angels prepared from the days of their fathers; therefore I need to commission an angel for them to guard them on the way — either another angel, or to commission upon them this angel who goes on behalf of Israel. And what He said — ״כִּי לֹא אֶעֱלֶה בְּקִרְבְּךָ״ etc. — that was on account of the mixed multitude, for they are a stiff-necked people; therefore I do not wish to go in their midst. And along with them He brought it to fulfillment according to the statement of the Gemara (Bava Kamma 92a), for they are the reason that I will not go in Myself also with you and with Israel." "<b>And Moses said to HaShem: See, You say to me: Lead this people.</b> Concerning the mixed multitude — not in the manner spoken concerning Israel (Exodus 3:17), ״וָאֹמַר אַעֲלֶה אֶתְכֶם מֵעֳנִי מִצְרַיִם״. Rather, You hung the bringing up upon me, to say: just as you brought them up from the land of Egypt of your own accord, so shall you bring them up from here — as it is written, ״לֵךְ עֲלֵה מִזֶּה אַתָּה וְהָעָם אֲשֶׁר הֶעֱלִיתָ״ etc., meaning, just as you brought them up from Egypt, so shall you bring them up from here. <b>And You have not made known to me whom You will send with me.</b> This matter — that You will send Your emissary with me — You have not made known to me. For what You said, ״וְשָׁלַחְתִּי מַלְאָךְ לְפָנֶיךָ״ — by this they are not distinguished at all from the rest of the nations, for every nation has a prince and an angel on high. And You should have had pity on my honor: since they went out by word of mouth from Egypt and cleaved to You, You should have distinguished them from all the peoples and gone in their midst. And even if you say that there is reason for the matter in what You said — ״כִּי לֹא אֶעֱלֶה בְּקִרְבְּךָ״ etc. — nevertheless You should have informed me that Your emissary would go with me at the very least, for I too need divine support and help to guide them in a straight path. And this angel sent before them is only to drive out the seven nations, and the main thing is missing from the book, for we still need instruction to know the way of HaShem according to the Torah." "<b>And You said: I have known you by name.</b> There are those who say that He said this because it is written ״וּשְׁמִי ה׳ לֹא נוֹדַעְתִּי לָהֶם״ (Exodus 6:3) — implying, but to you I made Myself known by My name YHWH; and according to our approach, he says it concerning the name אֶהְיֶה, as it is written (Exodus 3:12) ״כִּי אֶהְיֶה עִמָּךְ״ — the name אֶהְיֶה, He will be with you always, and this is what I need now. For at first You said, ״יְדַעְתִּיךָ בְשֵׁם״ — that HaShem Himself would be with me, and now You have withdrawn from this promise; and not only do You Yourself not wish to be with me, as it is written ״כִּי לֹא אֶעֱלֶה בְּקִרְבְּךָ״, but even Your emissary is not with me but only before me — for You have not made known to me whom You will send with me, and this is very far from the promise of ״יְדַעְתִּיךָ בְשֵׁם״. And concerning this Moses did not ask anything yet; he only spoke with wonderment — that You did not make known to me that You would send with me. Afterward he presented his request in the form of a proposal and said: ״וְגַם מָצָאתָ חֵן בְּעֵינָי״ — and if I already had occasion to ask for something You would do for me by virtue of finding favor, in any case I asked for nothing because the hour did not so require. Therefore he said: and now the hour has arrived that I need to ask for something, and it is — ״אִם נָא מָצָאתִי חֵן בְּעֵינֶיךָ הוֹדִיעֵנִי נָא אֶת דְּרָכֶיךָ״. And it is not enough that You make it known to me while I am brutish and do not understand, but ״וְאֵדָעֲךָ״ — give me a heart to understand, so that I may know Your way, in order that I may cause others to benefit from this; and it is that I find favor in Your eyes in addition to the first favor. And this is because through the knowledge of the intelligibilia the intellect cleaves to the Intellect and they become one — may my mouth be as Your mouth — and I will benefit from this, that from me the favor will also extend to others, which had not been so previously, for the favor extended only from You to me; and at the time when You make known to me Your way and grant me understanding, the intellect will cleave to the Intellect and the finding of favor will be added to me and will extend from me also to all those who cleave to me — and they are the mixed multitude — and through this they will be called Your people and not mine alone. And this is what he said: ״וּרְאֵה כִּי עַמְּךָ הַגּוֹי הַזֶּה״ — and do not say to me any more ״אֲשֶׁר הֶעֱלִיתָ״ etc."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 33, :notes {4 "The Aramaic idiom בַּהֲדֵי הוּצָא לָקֵי כְּרָבָא in comment [2] is rendered literally as 'along with them He brought it to fulfillment according to the statement of the Gemara'; the syntax is elliptical and the phrase is translated hyper-literally to preserve the compression of the original."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:25:37.079407404Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}